  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>MQH - An improper integral on an infinite interval</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the integral below and determine if it converges or diverges. 
</p>
<p>Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>.</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Recall that we can’t evaluate the integral as long as there is an infinity in one of the limits. Therefore, we’ll need to replace infinity with a parameter \(t\) as follows,
</p>
<p>The given improper integral is</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  I = \int_{@lower_limit@}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@func@} \, dx. \)
</p>

<p>We will first evaluate whether the integral converges or diverges by finding the limit of the integral as \( t \to \infty \):</p>

<p>
\( \displaystyle I =  \int_{@lower_limit@}^{{@t@}}{@func@} \, dx. \)
</p>
<p>To find antiderivative of \(\displaystyle {@func@}  \) we apply exponential rule: \( \displaystyle \int a^x \, dx = \frac{a^x}{\ln(a)} \,\) with \(a=e\)</p>
   <p>Hence  \( \displaystyle \int e^{x} \, dx = e^{x}\)</p>
   <p>\(\qquad \implies  \displaystyle  {@const@} \int {@fx@} \, dx = {@func@} \)</p>
<p>Evaluate limit points:</p>
<p>
\(
\begin{align}
\displaystyle 
\int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@t@}} {@func@} \, dx &= \Bigg[ {@func@} \Bigg]_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@t@}} \\
                              &= {@indef_val@} \\
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<p>Take the limit as \( t \to \infty \)</p>

<p>
\(\displaystyle  I(t) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left( {@indef_val@}\right) = {@ta_1@} \)
</p>

<p>The improper integral is {@mcq_correct(ta_4)[1]@}.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
